e overstated. A rendered value cannot introduce `&`, `<`, or `>`; therefore the integrity of an operator-authored delimiter is assured **only for delimiters composed of those characters**. Because operators own all prompt structure, the system cannot know which delimiter a template uses, and a delimiter such as `### SECTION` or `--- BEGIN ---` receives no protection at all. Operator documentation SHALL recommend an XML-style delimiter and the packaged default SHALL demonstrate one. A delimiter is defense-in-depth and a signal to the model, never an enforcement boundary.\n\nValues projected for **single-line** use SHALL have line breaks removed, so that a value cannot escape a line-oriented label. Values that are legitimately multi-line SHALL retain their line breaks and therefore require a block-shaped delimiter rather than an inline label; operator documentation SHALL state this distinction.\"\"\"\nassert old_esc in t\nt = t.replace(old_esc, new_esc, 1)\n\n# Replace the four blocklist scenarios with allowlist-shaped ones.\nold_scen_start = t.index(\"#### Scenario: Template requests unescaped output\")\nold_scen_end = t.index(\"#### Scenario: Legacy interpolation syntax is present\")\nt = t[:old_scen_start] + \"\"\"#### Scenario: Template requests unescaped output\n\n- **WHEN** a configured prompt file emits a value through unescaped output\n- **THEN** startup fails naming the model id and that expression\n- **AND** the template is not loaded with escaping bypassed\n\n#### Scenario: Template references a fragment in any form\n\n- **WHEN** a configured prompt file uses a partial reference, a partial with a fallback block, a decorator, or an inline-partial decorator block\n- **THEN** startup fails in every one of those cases, naming the model id and the construct\n- **AND** no file-include or prompt-fragment mechanism becomes reachable\n\n#### Scenario: An unrecognized node kind is rejected by default\n\n- **WHEN** a configured prompt file contains a node kind outside